#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192304 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ae613d4-e3fe-53cb-b7b0-61596df291d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Sums and Products"}] :question/id #uuid "b8e874e4-eb17-55f3-a0dc-c70405e45d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-10868" :question/problem "Find the product of all three cube roots of unity." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the principal cube root of unity is\n\n$$\nz=e^{2\\pi\\text{i}/3}\n$$\n\nand that any cube root of unity is of the form $z^k$ for $k=0,1,2$. Also, we have $z^3=1$.\n\nTherefore, the product of all three roots is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{0} \\cdot z^{1} \\cdot z^{2} &= z^{0 + 1 + 2} \\\\\n&= z^{3} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "75d210b5-6ca5-598c-ad5e-59d10cf59e5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10879" :question/problem "Let $z$ be a $7$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $z^7 = 1$\n2. $(z+z)^7 = 1$\n3. $z+z=2z$ is a $7$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $z$ is a $7$th root of unity, then (by definition), we have that\n$z^7=1$.\n- Statement II is false. We have\n$(z + z)^{7}|= (2z)^{7}; = 2^{7} \\cdot z^{7}; = 128 \\cdot 1; = 128; \\ne 1$.\n- Statement III is false. From statement II, we have that $(z+z)^7 \\neq 1$, which means that $z+z=2z$ is *not* a $7$th root of unity. In general, the sum of two $n$th roots of unity is *not* an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192308 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab23d8f-dc62-5862-b5e9-e7d5602ad42c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Root of Unity Using Its Principal Root"}] :question/id #uuid "b7dfa5f4-f774-520f-9107-e832aede7692" :question/math-academy-id "e-10880" :question/problem "The $12$th root of unity $e^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}$ can be written as\n$e^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3} = z^p$,\nwhere $z$ is the principal $12$th root of unity. What is the value of $p$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $12$th roots of unity can be written in the form\n\n$$\ne^{2 k\\pi \\text{i} / 12}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk=0,1,\\ldots,11\n$$\n\nWe obtain the *principal* $12$th root of unity by substituting $k=1{:}$\n\n$$\nz= e^{2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\pi \\text{i} / 12} = {\\color{blue}e^{\\pi \\text{i} / 6}}\n$$\n\nWe can write the given root of unity as a power of the principal root as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2π\\textrm{i}/3} &= e^{(2 \\cdot 2π)\\textrm{i}/(2 \\cdot 3)} \\\\\n&= e^{4π\\textrm{i}/6} \\\\\n&= e^{4 \\cdot π\\textrm{i}/6} \\\\\n&= (e^{π\\textrm{i}/6})^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\np={\\color{red}4}\n$$"}]}
